  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nMain focus today is the impending convective potential. Modified\nobserved and model forecast soundings show instability is\nextremely limited currently, with a small unstable zone just \nabove the surface boundary layer. Large scale jet-level ascent\nis approaching from the west but will have a lot of work to do\nto steepen lapse rates in the 1.8\" PWAT airmass that originated\nin an east Pacific tropical cyclone. The air advecting into the\narea currently on these gusty south to southeast winds is also\npretty homogeneous to what we already have in place although \nsome insolation is warming it up. This heating though is having\nthe effect of mixing lower dewpoints in. Richer low level \nmoisture is present in eastern Arkansas and surface winds shift\naround to the south southwest just ahead of the approaching \nfront/convective line that is modeled by available CAMS. \n\nHRRR and RRFS1 average out to about 80 J/kg of entrainment\nfactored MLCAPE in the 18 and 15z runs even with that slight \nuptick in moisture/instability. Shear is quite good as this area\nmoves through with deep layer at about 50 kts or so and 0-1km \nshear line parallel at about 22-25 kts. If we can get enough \ninstability for surface-based convection there would be a wind \nand QLCS tornado threat. Based on the timing of solar \nheating/advection and the trough it still looks like southeast \nMissouri has the best chance to overachieve on instability and \nyield some threat. Although the most likely outcome for now \nlooks like a weaker partially elevated line of convection or \nheavy showers that passes fairly harmlessly. I'd say right now \nabout a 15-20% chance for a warning or two to be necessary in \nSEMO and maybe 5-10% elsewhere. Areas of light to moderate rain \nlook to follow any convection. PWATs/rainfall efficiency should \nbe high but the intensity and duration of convection for now \nstill looks to limit flood potential.\n\nCold front behind all this comes through tonight cooling us off\na little. Another cold front is then progged for Friday morning\nthat cools us off even further and give us weekend lows close to\nfreezing. Moisture/rainfall potential with this second front\nstill appears low.",
